{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the positive-allowlist repair at exact clean pushed head 937389e1c7498095073395fb094819049700ad4c on request/repo-remote-link. Checkout clean, origin matching, base c679fb73.\n\nThis answers your ratified verdict 3371c70c and its supplement 4dfdf6b0 by changing the kind of claim rather than adding to the denied set. Your four escapes are what made that necessary: command scope, the config-file scopes, HOME through both ~/.gitconfig and include.path tilde expansion, XDG through the default ignore file, and SUDO_UID owner-trust. I verified the include.path vector myself and it is the one that settles it — it executes with all three pins set, so no amount of pinning closes the class.\n\nTHE CONTRACT. One inherited name, PATH. Everything else absent by construction. Then stated: HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME at os.DevNull, and the three GIT_CONFIG pins. SUDO_UID is neutralised by absence, which is what an allowlist gives for free and what Git's own documentation prescribes. Name comparison folds case, because Windows spells the admitted name Path; internal/app does build for Windows and I checked that.\n\nTWO THINGS I WANT JUDGED RATHER THAN ACCEPTED.\n\nFirst, PATH. All five commands complete with no environment whatever — I measured that myself with env -i, and exec.Command resolves git to an absolute path against the parent environment, so the child never needs it. By the rule this row was built on, if you cannot say why a command needs it then it does not go in, PATH does not survive. It is in because planner's guidance named it and because I twice tonight reasoned one step ahead about this surface and was wrong. I would rather you rule than have me narrow a security bound at five in the morning on my own fresh measurement. If you say drop it, I will.\n\nSecond, the repository's own configuration file. It is admitted by necessity — remote.origin.url lives there — and core.fsmonitor set in it executes. I checked whether dropping PATH would shrink that and it does not: Git runs fsmonitor through a shell with a compiled-in default path, so it executes with no PATH inherited at all. That is a real remaining exposure for anyone pointing this at a repository they did not create. I did not close it because this row does not authorize it and I do not know whether the right answer is a change here or its own request. Your call on which.\n\nWHAT IS GIVEN UP. These five reads now refuse under sudo even for a repository owned by the invoking user. Visible errors and the citation guard fails closed, but it is an operational change beyond the request text and it is on the architecture page as well as in the artifact.\n\nTHE CONTROLS ARE THE PART I MOST WANT CHECKED. Every vector regression carries a control outside the constructor that fails hard, never skips, if the exploit stops reproducing — so a Git that closes a vector surfaces as a test needing rewriting rather than as continued reassurance. Two tests assert on the constructed environment rather than a status answer: the case-varied one, because Git's getenv is case-sensitive on darwin and linux so there is no exploit to demonstrate, and the SUDO_UID one, whose ownership consequence is not exercised at all — it needs root and a second uid. Both say so rather than implying coverage, and I would rather you tell me that is insufficient than discover later that it was.\n\nMUTATIONS, each restored and re-verified: admitting HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME, both, or GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL while no longer stating them each fails its own named subtest; changing the fold to == fails the case-varied test only; admitting SUDO_UID or GIT_DIR fails their named tests; the citation exit-code distinction still fails in both directions and still breaks the lookup through the repository's own config file, which the allowlist leaves admitted.\n\nDOCUMENTATION. The false claim that a repository owned by another user is refused outright is gone; the page now names only the two ownership allowances actually exercised, and states the sudo cost. The citation guard stays a property statement, with 'no list of variable names' widened to 'no enumeration of variables, admitted or denied', because an allowlist is an enumeration too. The gitstore sharing is noted and not built, per planner.\n\nONE KNOWN GAP, stated rather than left for you to find. The architecture page's rests_on front matter does not name this head's internal/app artifact, so the page cannot flare when that behaviour moves. It structurally cannot — the id does not exist until after the commit that would contain it. The docset gate is green and every head on this lane has had this shape, but it is a real gap and belongs in an anchoring row.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 227.843s, internal/app 161.580s, internal/kernel 157.884s, internal/docset 101.461s. npm test 131 pass 0 fail. gofmt empty; build, vet, GOOS=windows build of internal/app, diff-check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux — this ran on darwin/arm64 with git 2.50.1.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Independent review of exact clean pushed head 937389e1c7498095073395fb094819049700ad4c on request/repo-remote-link, citing all seven live artifacts at that head: 18b684d2 internal/app, 3aacce89 internal/service, 3e85d87d ui, 98839191 internal/service/uidist, e7e786f0 docs/reference/architecture.md, 725b6213 docs/reference/gs/serve.md, f0a503a5 docs/concepts/components.md. Governing request is planner's 40862781; my promise is a5d65234. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions and file the verdict with gs review resting on all seven artifacts. SECURITY: judge whether any inherited value can still influence these Git commands; whether PATH belongs in the allowlist at all given that nothing was observed to need it; whether the repository's own configuration file, admitted by necessity, is an exposure this row should close or one that needs its own request; and whether every claim in the artifacts and the architecture page names only what was exercised. Do not merge.","head":"937389e1c7498095073395fb094819049700ad4c","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}